A: You are having trouble with the PexAssumeUnderTest, so I would recommend refactoring your test method as follows:
PexMethod, PexAssemblyUnderTest and PexAllowedException may be omitted:
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_GetContract(int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);
    // the rest of your method
}

Also, please make sure to mark your fields as PexInSourceAssembly to allow Pex to understand the assembly:
[PexInSourceAssembly]
ArrayList arrList;

You should also specify the assembly you want Pex to test as PexAssemblyUnderTest:
[PexMethod, PexAssemblyUnderTest("System.Collections")]

It should look something like this:
[PexMethod, PexAssemblyUnderTest("System.Collections")]
public void PUT_GetContract(int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);
    // the rest of your method
}

In addition, since Pex does not interact directly with .Net framework classes as far as I know, but it works better with a similar type of control over your test, you might want to consider using another fuzz testing tool than Pex.
Another thing to look at is the setup of your ArrayList. Make sure that your ArrayList is being populated with data before your method under test is being tested.
Lastly, to debug a more specific issue, consider looking at the output pex.log file. It provides much better insight into why your test is failing.

A: Since ArrayList class does not fully implement IList interface, the Pex-runner is not able to handle all its possible behaviors. That makes it difficult to provide a specific test. I suggest to use other types of collection such as List or Dictionary instead. 
Note: Pex may still miss some edge cases since it's focused primarily on the paths that your method under test might take, but it may provide better coverage for collections which have more known behaviors.  
Refer to this question: 
Pex not able to deal with specific .NET collections like ArrayList 
and related posts for more information regarding the same.

A: The issue you're having appears to be associated with the way Pex interacts with .Net framework classes, particularly the ArrayList class. Pex cannot properly handle some behaviors of ArrayList, like for instance, when elements are removed. 
As a result, it's a bit difficult to provide a specific test case, as it would be more likely to result in issues rather than in a test that could help you.
In your specific case, I don't see any obvious ways to improve your test case, or even to find an equivalent situation that Pex can handle, since it hasn't been designed to work with ArrayList. 

