Please note that the original `arrList` has to be empty (`Count==0`) before the test, and the index needs to be in range for the test to succeed.

This test tries to put a value at a specific index and then check the returned value, and it checks the `Count` of the updated `arrList` and also if the value returned is in the `arrList` or not. It also checks if the new `Count` of `arrList` is as expected by the count of the old `arrList` increased by one.

One point to remember here is that `ArrayList` may behave differently in different environments. For instance, when using `Pex`, `ArrayList`'s `Count` property does not always reflect the actual number of elements that are stored in the array. It could also provide some garbage values. Therefore, the exact value of `Count` when the assertion is made might not be what you expect for debugging purpose. You need to figure out an appropriate way to handle it, like ignoring or handling the specific parts of array lists based on your specific requirements.

There are lots of other ways you can further refine the test to meet your specific needs, such as testing with different kinds of objects, checking exception cases, and other edge cases.

You can also consider replacing `ArrayList` with a more appropriate data structure, such as a `List<T>`, to get more precise tests and better coverage. Keep in mind that your team's expertise and the specific requirements of your project should guide this decision.

Please note that this test might be too rigorous and will provide a lot of false positives during the test, which will affect the effectiveness of your testing and debugging workflow. I strongly suggest you to adjust the test appropriately based on your needs and specific scenarios to ensure the effectiveness.

This test was generated by Microsoft's Visual Studio 2017 and can be executed using Pex's playback feature to see the results.

Also, in Visual Studio 2017, you might want to install and enable the Pex extension for better support of these sort of tests.

The provided test code doesn't handle negative indices, you might need to adjust or extend it to cover that.

Remember that adding test logic increases the overall complexity of your tests and might make them harder to maintain and read. It should be implemented for your specific scenarios and requirements.

