using System;
using System.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.Psi;
using Microsoft.Psi.Components;
using Microsoft.Psi.Data;

namespace Microsoft.Psi.Persistence
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Represents a subscriber that can be used to write to an existing piece of data to store.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// This subscriber should typically be coupled with a publisher (or a computation pipeline that generates data)
    /// in a Psi workflow. The subscriber is responsible for managing the store as well as subscribing to the
    /// store and writing the data messages to the store. Writing of data also should be done using
    /// <see cref="PersistenceWriter"/> methods.
    /// </remarks>
    public abstract class PersistenceSubscriber : IDisposable, IPipelineNotificationReceiver
    {
        private bool isOpen;

        /// <summary>
        /// Initializes a new instance of the <see cref="PersistenceSubscriber"/> class.
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="name">The name.</param>
        public PersistenceSubscriber(string name = "PersistenceSubscriber")
        {
            this.Name = name;
            this.Pipeline = null;
            this.Store = null;
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Occurs when the pipeline is about to initialize or being closing.
        /// </summary>
        public event PipelineInitializingEventHandler PipelineInitializing;

        /// <summary>
        /// Occurs when the pipeline has finished initializing or is closing.
        /// </summary>
        public event PipelineInitializedEventHandler PipelineInitialized;

        /// <summary>
        /// Gets the pipeline.
        /// </summary>
        public IPipeline Pipeline { get; private set; }

        /// <summary>
        /// Gets the name.
        /// </summary>
        public string Name { get; private set; }

        /// <summary>
        /// Gets or sets the store.
        /// </summary>
        public IPersistenceStore Store { get; protected set; }

        /// <summary>
        /// Opens this instance.
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="pipeline">The pipeline.</param>
        public void Open(IPipeline pipeline)
        {
            this.Pipeline = pipeline ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(pipeline));
            this.Pipeline.NotificationReceivers.Add(this);
            this.OnOpening();
            this.isOpen = true;
            this.OnOpened();
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Called when the subscriber is opening.
        /// </summary>
        protected virtual void OnOpening()
        {
            this.PipelineInitializing?.Invoke(this, new PipelineInitializingEventArgs(this.Pipeline, PipelinePhase.PreInitialization));
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Called when the subscriber is opened.
        /// </summary>
        protected virtual void OnOpened()
        {
            this.PipelineInitialized?.Invoke(this, new PipelineInitializedEventArgs(this.Pipeline, PipelinePhase.PreInitialization));
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Performs application-defined tasks associated with--------------------	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ index passed in is not less than zero
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ index passed in is not more than the old array size

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that new array list count equals old array list count minus one
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that new result contains in array
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that new array list count doesn't equals old array list count

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ return value is not equal to default of the specific type
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ return value of the specific type greater than or equals to minimal possible value of that type
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ return value of the specific type less than or equals to maximum possible value of that type
        }
    }
}
```
This example shows how you could add additional assertions or assumptions to your test for the `Get` method of the contract.

Notice that for each assertion or assumption, you should replace '@@@' with the variables for which you need to make assumptions or assertions. You should also replace '@@@ some condition' with the condition you would want to include in your test. You should also replace '@@@' with the part of the method signature where you replaced the placeholder 'method under test name'. The conditions are written in the style of C# syntax for asserts. 

This PEX style contract test checks that you can get an element from an array list using a specified index without altering the state of the array list. 

It also assumes that the provided index is in valid range(not less than 0 and not greater than array list size).

It observes the values for viewing in the PEG Visualizer and checks a wide variety of conditions for the tested method to pass the test.

As PEX tests go, these are quite compact and focus on validating single method from the contract's specification.

Note: In addition to the provided information, `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);` in the PEX contract test was used for the purpose of assuming this pre-condition. It is necessary as the index we are accessing is within the range of the count of ArrayList object.
}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[containsverbatim]
\frametitle{Contract Testing}
\framesubtitle{Continued}

\begin{scriptsize}
\begin{verbatim}
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
    public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrLIst, int index)
    {
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);
        int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int Old_index = index;

        object Old_ElementAtIndex = arrList[index];

        int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        bool New_containsOldElement = arrList.Contains(Old_ElementAtIndex);

        // Assertions 
        Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
        Debug.Assert(New_containsOldElement);
        Debug.Assert(Old_index > -1 && Old_index < Old_arrListCount);
        Debug.Assert(Old_index == index);
    }
}
\end{verbatim}
\end{scriptsize}

This contract test PUT_GetContract asserts that:

  \begin{itemize}
    \item An old array list count is equal to a new array list count
    \item A contains method from the array list class is used to confirm array list contains old element at index
    \item The index passed in is not less than zero and the index passed in is not more than old array list size
    \item The index passed is exactly the same as the old index passed in
  \end{itemize}

This shows that you can get an element from an ArrayList without making any changes to the collection. If the index used is not within range of the collection, it will fall under the assumption in PEG. 
\end{frame}

